    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d90">

                <pb facs="0162" n="162" sameAs="frus:frus1861/pg162"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Adams to Mr. Seward</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 58.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the
                            United States</hi>,<lb/><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >London</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1861-10-11"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >October</hi> 11, 1861</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose a copy of Lord
                    Russell’s note to me of the 4th instant, in reply to my representation of the
                    conduct of the authorities of the island of Trinidad, made under instructions
                    from the department.</p>
                <p>It will appear from this that the governor of Trinidad, by hoisting the British
                    flag on the government flag-staff, probably desired to signify to the officers
                    of the Sumter, on their arrival, the neutral character of the island, but that
                    he in the meantime forgot that the act is susceptible of a very different
                    construction in the popular mind.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * </p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer><signed>CHARLES FRANCIS ADAMS.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, Washington, D. C.</hi></p>
                </postscript>


                <xi:include href="d90-a1.xml" xpointer="d90-a1"/>
